Box: a slow viewer gets fewer pictures instead of an ever-growing lag (2026-10-12).

On the road some wired sessions lagged for ten-odd seconds until a replug. At home, a page reading at
4 Mbit/s against about 9 Mbit/s of picture fell 11 s behind in 20 s and stayed there; nothing was ever
dropped, as the backlog sat below the per-viewer send queue (Ktor, Netty, TLS, kernel). The box now marks
the stream every 200 ms with the bytes queued so far, the page answers each mark at once, and LagGate lets
a picture frame out only while what is still on its way would reach the page within 300 ms at the rate it
has been taking in; otherwise the frame is dropped and the picture restarts at a keyframe. Sound and control
messages always go out, and a page that never answers is never held back. The viewer socket's send buffer
is fixed at 128 KiB. Same test afterwards: mostly under 0.5 s, peaks 0.8 s, 1 s for a moment when the
rate first drops; back to a few ms as soon as the link recovers.

